1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,580
In the year 1386, in the French town

2
00:00:03,580 --> 00:00:06,960
of Falaise, a crowd packed the main square

3
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:08,760
to watch an execution.

4
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:11,260
The prisoner had been tried in court, and

5
00:00:11,260 --> 00:00:13,080
found guilty of killing a child.

6
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:14,980
The judge had read out the sentence.

7
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:18,100
And then the prisoner was led out, dressed

8
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:20,240
in a man's jacket and breeches.

9
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:22,320
The prisoner... was a pig.

10
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:25,060
For hundreds of years, all across Europe,

11
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:27,140
animals were taken to court.

12
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:29,680
Pigs. Bulls. Rats.

13
00:00:29,980 --> 00:00:30,760
Even insects.

14
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:31,760
They got lawyers.

15
00:00:32,440 --> 00:00:33,460
They got official summons.

16
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:35,920
Some were even offered their own land.

17
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:38,760
So why on earth would anyone put an

18
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:39,920
animal on trial?

19
00:00:40,570 --> 00:00:41,950
The real answer has less to do with

20
00:00:41,950 --> 00:00:43,950
animals... and a lot more to do with

21
00:00:43,950 --> 00:00:44,650
money.

22
00:00:44,990 --> 00:00:47,290
I'm Wy, and this is the true story

23
00:00:47,290 --> 00:00:48,570
of the animal trials.

24
00:00:51,370 --> 00:00:53,510
A sow, a female pig, had attacked a

25
00:00:53,510 --> 00:00:55,570
small child, and the child had died.

26
00:00:55,970 --> 00:00:57,690
Today, we'd call that a terrible accident.

27
00:00:58,070 --> 00:01:00,750
In 1386, the town called it murder.

28
00:01:01,150 --> 00:01:02,590
So the sow was put on trial in

29
00:01:02,590 --> 00:01:04,630
the town's criminal court, the same court

30
00:01:04,630 --> 00:01:05,570
that tried people.

31
00:01:05,970 --> 00:01:07,790
And the judge's sentence was an eye for

32
00:01:07,790 --> 00:01:08,490
an eye.

33
00:01:08,810 --> 00:01:10,330
The sow would be wounded in the head

34
00:01:10,330 --> 00:01:12,930
and front legs, just like the child, and

35
00:01:12,930 --> 00:01:13,630
then hanged.

36
00:01:13,910 --> 00:01:15,610
On the day of the execution, she was

37
00:01:15,610 --> 00:01:17,350
dressed in a man's clothes and led to

38
00:01:17,350 --> 00:01:18,990
the square beside the town hall.

39
00:01:19,390 --> 00:01:21,270
The hangman was paid ten sous and ten

40
00:01:21,270 --> 00:01:22,390
deniers...

41
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:24,460
plus a brand new pair of gloves.

42
00:01:24,860 --> 00:01:26,720
Why gloves? So he could leave with clean

43
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:27,420
hands.

44
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:28,300
He wasn't a butcher.

45
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:30,280
He was an officer of the law, doing

46
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:31,160
the court's work.

47
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:33,200
A painting of the execution was even put

48
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:34,860
up on the wall of the town's church.

49
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:36,380
And here's the strange part.

50
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:39,020
Falaise wasn't unusual at all.

51
00:01:39,420 --> 00:01:41,180
Pigs roamed freely through medieval towns,

52
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:42,820
and they turn up in these trials more

53
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:43,860
than any other animal.

54
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:48,780
Group one: big animals that hurt or killed

55
00:01:48,780 --> 00:01:51,800
a person. Pigs, bulls, horses, dogs.

56
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:53,500
They were tried one at a time by

57
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:56,000
ordinary judges. And if found guilty, they

58
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:57,160
were usually put to death.

59
00:01:57,560 --> 00:01:59,660
Group two was much stranger.

60
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:02,640
Rats. Mice. Locusts.

61
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:03,420
Beetles.

62
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:04,760
Pests that ate the crops.

63
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:06,720
You can't arrest ten thousand rats.

64
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:08,940
So these cases went to a different court:

65
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:10,080
the church.

66
00:02:10,420 --> 00:02:11,920
And the church had a weapon no ordinary

67
00:02:11,920 --> 00:02:15,720
judge had: a solemn curse, called an

68
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:18,040
anathema, ordering the creatures to leave.

69
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:20,680
But before any curse, the accused deserved

70
00:02:20,680 --> 00:02:21,420
a fair trial.

71
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:23,520
And a fair trial needs a lawyer.

72
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:28,900
In the early 1500s, around the French city

73
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:31,720
of Autun, rats destroyed the barley crop.

74
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:33,520
The church court ordered the rats to

75
00:02:33,520 --> 00:02:36,080
appear. And, as the story goes, it gave

76
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:37,460
them a defence lawyer:

77
00:02:37,860 --> 00:02:39,940
a young legal expert named Bartholomew

78
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:40,800
Chassenée.

79
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:43,840
Chassenée took the case very seriously.

80
00:02:44,240 --> 00:02:45,920
First, he argued that his clients lived

81
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:48,120
scattered across many villages, so one

82
00:02:48,120 --> 00:02:49,200
summons wasn't fair.

83
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:51,300
The court agreed, and ordered a new

84
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:52,840
summons, read aloud in every parish

85
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:53,540
church.

86
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:55,720
The rats still didn't show up.

87
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:57,840
So Chassenée found a better excuse.

88
00:02:58,240 --> 00:02:59,700
His clients wanted to come...

89
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:02,260
but the journey was far too dangerous.

90
00:03:02,660 --> 00:03:04,360
The roads were full of cats, watching

91
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:06,460
their every move, waiting at every corner.

92
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:08,500
And no one, he argued, can be forced

93
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:09,940
to come to court if the trip puts

94
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:10,980
their life at risk.

95
00:03:11,380 --> 00:03:12,700
Nobody knows what the judge finally

96
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:14,900
decided. But the case made Chassenée

97
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:15,680
famous.

98
00:03:16,080 --> 00:03:17,520
Years later, when he was a senior judge

99
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:19,540
himself, a colleague reminded him of those

100
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:21,940
rats. If even rats deserved a defence,

101
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:23,540
surely people did too.

102
00:03:24,260 --> 00:03:24,960
Chassenée agreed.

103
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:30,040
Not every case was this funny.

104
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:33,660
In 1474, in the Swiss city of Basel,

105
00:03:34,300 --> 00:03:37,700
judges sentenced a rooster to be burned...

106
00:03:38,270 --> 00:03:40,390
for the crime of laying an egg.

107
00:03:40,790 --> 00:03:42,290
To us, that sounds ridiculous.

108
00:03:43,110 --> 00:03:45,630
To the people of Basel, it was terrifying.

109
00:03:46,030 --> 00:03:47,930
Legend said an egg laid by a rooster

110
00:03:47,930 --> 00:03:50,590
could hatch a basilisk: a monster that

111
00:03:50,590 --> 00:03:52,550
could kill with a single look.

112
00:03:52,950 --> 00:03:55,870
Witches were said to use these eggs in

113
00:03:55,870 --> 00:03:56,557
their potions.

114
00:03:56,557 --> 00:03:57,890
So the rooster was burned on a hill

115
00:03:57,890 --> 00:04:00,250
outside the city, in front of a huge

116
00:04:00,250 --> 00:04:00,950
crowd.

117
00:04:01,230 --> 00:04:03,670
Today, scientists know that, very rarely,

118
00:04:03,970 --> 00:04:06,070
a hen's body can change, so she looks

119
00:04:06,070 --> 00:04:07,790
and even crows like a rooster.

120
00:04:08,190 --> 00:04:10,890
But in 1474, nobody knew that.

121
00:04:11,490 --> 00:04:13,670
They only saw a monster in the making.

122
00:04:16,390 --> 00:04:19,230
Now for the strangest trial of all.

123
00:04:19,630 --> 00:04:21,170
High in the Alps, there's a village called

124
00:04:21,170 --> 00:04:23,530
Saint-Julien, famous for its wine.

125
00:04:23,930 --> 00:04:27,050
In 1545, tiny green beetles called weevils

126
00:04:27,050 --> 00:04:28,690
began destroying the vines.

127
00:04:29,090 --> 00:04:30,690
The villagers took the weevils to the

128
00:04:30,690 --> 00:04:32,830
church court. And the weevils got two

129
00:04:32,830 --> 00:04:33,530
lawyers.

130
00:04:33,730 --> 00:04:35,830
But the judge said something surprising.

131
00:04:36,230 --> 00:04:38,390
God made the plants for insects too, he

132
00:04:38,390 --> 00:04:40,350
said. So instead of punishing them,

133
00:04:40,630 --> 00:04:42,430
everyone should pray and say sorry for

134
00:04:42,430 --> 00:04:43,130
their sins...

135
00:04:43,420 --> 00:04:46,040
and, above all, pay their tithes.

136
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:47,080
The village prayed.

137
00:04:47,820 --> 00:04:49,820
And soon after, the weevils were gone.

138
00:04:50,220 --> 00:04:52,840
Decades later, in 1587, they came back.

139
00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:54,760
And the villagers went back to court.

140
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:56,660
This time, they tried a deal.

141
00:04:57,060 --> 00:04:58,620
At a public meeting in the village square,

142
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:00,620
they voted to give the weevils their very

143
00:05:00,620 --> 00:05:02,760
own piece of land, outside the vineyards.

144
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:04,620
The villagers even kept the right to walk

145
00:05:04,620 --> 00:05:06,460
through it, as long as they didn't spoil

146
00:05:06,460 --> 00:05:07,300
the weevils' food.

147
00:05:07,520 --> 00:05:10,380
But the weevils' lawyer turned the offer

148
00:05:10,380 --> 00:05:12,920
down. The land, he said, was too poor

149
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:13,680
for his clients.

150
00:05:14,080 --> 00:05:16,240
So the judge sent experts to inspect it.

151
00:05:16,640 --> 00:05:17,580
And the final verdict?

152
00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:19,180
Nobody knows.

153
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:21,120
The last page of the court record was

154
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:24,100
destroyed... by rats or insects.

155
00:05:26,820 --> 00:05:29,200
So why did people do all this?

156
00:05:29,850 --> 00:05:32,170
For dangerous farm animals, one idea is

157
00:05:32,170 --> 00:05:34,390
simple. When something terrible happened,

158
00:05:34,730 --> 00:05:36,710
a trial put the world back in order.

159
00:05:37,170 --> 00:05:38,790
Someone, or something, was held

160
00:05:38,790 --> 00:05:41,510
responsible, in public, by the rules.

161
00:05:41,910 --> 00:05:44,150
For pests, the answer may be money.

162
00:05:44,750 --> 00:05:46,370
Remember that judge, telling people to pay

163
00:05:46,370 --> 00:05:47,110
their tithes?

164
00:05:47,760 --> 00:05:49,180
A tithe was a tenth of what you

165
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:50,460
grew, given to the church.

166
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:52,280
And in some places, people had stopped

167
00:05:52,280 --> 00:05:52,920
paying.

168
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:56,420
In 2013, the economist Peter Leeson argued

169
00:05:56,420 --> 00:05:58,320
that pest trials helped the church win

170
00:05:58,320 --> 00:05:59,440
those tithes back.

171
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:00,540
Here's the trick.

172
00:06:00,940 --> 00:06:02,300
Pests come and go on their own.

173
00:06:02,700 --> 00:06:04,200
If the church cursed the pests and they

174
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:05,860
left, the curse looked real.

175
00:06:06,260 --> 00:06:08,500
If they stayed, people must be sinning.

176
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:10,600
Like, say, not paying their tithes.

177
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:12,640
Either way, the church's power looked

178
00:06:12,640 --> 00:06:14,440
stronger, and more people paid.

179
00:06:14,840 --> 00:06:16,340
So the trials weren't really about the

180
00:06:16,340 --> 00:06:17,080
animals at all.

181
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:18,980
They were about what people believed.

182
00:06:21,700 --> 00:06:24,720
Animal trials slowly faded as new ideas

183
00:06:24,720 --> 00:06:26,620
about science and the law spread across

184
00:06:26,620 --> 00:06:27,320
Europe.

185
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:28,960
But they lasted longer than you might

186
00:06:28,960 --> 00:06:31,160
think. Some were still being held in the

187
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:32,590
1700s.

188
00:06:32,980 --> 00:06:34,840
So next time your dog eats your

189
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:37,020
homework... be glad it doesn't need a

190
00:06:37,020 --> 00:06:37,720
lawyer.

191
00:06:37,860 --> 00:06:40,080
I'm Wy. Stay curious, and I'll see you

192
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:41,340
in the next true why.
